  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual summary of how stronger, more frequent heatwaves can develop through interacting atmospheric drivers and land–atmosphere feedbacks. At the center is a heatwave over land, shown as being intensified by warm advection, sinking air under a high-pressure blocking pattern, and greater incoming shortwave radiation under clearer skies. A Rossby wave train and blocking setup appear to favor more stationary weather, while moist ascending air and heavier precipitation occur elsewhere along the wave pattern, emphasizing a contrast between wet and dry regions.\n\nThe land surface plays a major role in amplifying the heat. Drying soil moisture is linked to reduced latent heat flux and increased sensible heating, which feeds back positively on near-surface temperature. Heatwaves also feed back on soil moisture by increasing desiccation, reinforcing the event. Cloud cover is shown as a negative influence on incoming solar radiation, whereas reduced cloudiness allows more sunlight to reach the surface and intensify heating.\n\nOverall, the graphic conveys that future heatwaves are not driven by a single cause but by a combination of large-scale circulation changes, clearer and more persistent blocking conditions, and self-reinforcing soil-moisture and energy-flux feedbacks.",
